  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Benign weather continues through the remainder \nof October with near to above normal temperatures expected. \n\nA weak front to our south will retreat northward as a warm front\nWednesday. Sprawling surface high pressure will move east from\nNew England but continue to extend southwestward into the\nsoutheastern states as it does so. Expect increasing clouds late\ntonight through Wednesday from two sources. A shortwave\napproaching from the west will bring mid and high level clouds.\nMeanwhile onshore flow will result in the development of low\nlevel clouds late tonight through midday Wednesday. While the\ncoverage of those clouds are uncertain, some fog could develop\ntoward dawn in any areas that are not seeing cloud cover.\nCurrent guidance favors this occurring in the vicinity of the\nI-95 corridor or Virginia piedmont. On Wednesday, expect lower\ntemperatures in the 60s due to the persistent cloud cover.\n\nAnother round of fog and/or low clouds may develop Wednesday\nnight in the stagnant low level airmass. However, clearing\nshould occur more quickly by Thursday thanks to southerly low\nlevel flow and westerly flow aloft. Temperatures will respond\naccordingly, as many areas reach the 70s.\n\nOvernight lows will be above normal through this stretch as\nwell, remaining in the 40s to lower 50s.\n\nAnother dry cold front will drop into the area Friday, but\ncooler air will be slow to arrive. Friday could be the warmest\nday of the week for many areas with highs in the mid to upper\n70s. High pressure will build from the Great Lakes to New\nEngland behind the front Friday night and Saturday. Highs on\nSaturday will be lower in the mid 60s, though still mild for\nthis time of year. Expect increasing cloud cover on Saturday as\nshortwave troughs approach from the southwest and warm advection\ncommences on the back side of the high.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...On-off rain showers are possible beginning Sunday\ninto early next week.\n\nA broad area of low pressure will form over the Midwest and \nthen track east late in the weekend, bringing increasing cloud \ncover to the region throughout the day Saturday. A stemming cold\nfront will cause temperatures to drop Saturday as this system \ntracks through the Mid-Atlantic, with potential rain showers \nbeginning on Sunday. Uncertainty remains high on total spread \nand rainfall accumulation as the system looks somewhat \ndisorganized in model guidance. Regardless, this will be the \nfirst chance for rain in well over a week across the region. \nWhile temperatures remain into the upper 60s Sunday and Monday,\nthis remains somewhat above average for the start of November. \n\nAs this system tracks offshore by Tuesday, a second, potentially \nmore potent upper-level low could form over the Plains and make its \nway to the region by the middle of next week. Uncertainty remains \nhigh regarding the track of this system, but this could bring \nadditional rainfall to the region as we get into November.",
